#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""devloop-auto-mode-question: whether the question on the rule for the
classifier of auto mode is put at a start with --auto, and the record of the
refusals that bring it.

Since 22 October 2026, the roadmap entry of that date on the question after a
refusal of auto mode. The question of text 4 of the roadmap entry of 9 October
2026 - may devloop enter the rule for the classifier into the person's
settings - is put at a first setup after the yes to the unattended mode, in
setup-checks step 8, and otherwise only at a start with --auto, with the
person there, where two things hold together: the rule does not stand in
their settings, or the settings cannot be read for it, and the project
carries an open issue for an install that auto mode refused in a run without
them. Nothing is stored on a no: while such an issue is open the question
comes at every start with --auto, and once the issue is closed it ends with
no further step. A refusal counts where auto mode refused, with a verdict of
its classifier, a command the install guard reads as an install by one of
the ways the rule names, in the spelling the rule writes - since 23 October
2026 brew install, go install, npm install -g, pnpm add -g, yarn global add,
bun add -g, cargo install, gem install, pipx install, uv tool install, pip
install in every form the guard reads as pip, playwright install, cypress
install, puppeteer browsers install, until that day the five tools and three
browsers of the rule's earlier wording - and nothing on the command stays
the person's under every answer; a denial whose input carries no reason, or
an empty one, names no verdict; hooks/pre-tool-use-install-guard.sh decides
that at the event PermissionDenied with its own reading and records it
through this program.
The rule can stand in the person's settings alone: the page "Configure auto
mode" of Claude Code, read on 22 October 2026, says the classifier does not
read autoMode from a project's .claude/settings.json or settings.local.json.

Usage
    devloop-auto-mode-question [DIR]
        decides the question. Exit 0 where it is to be put, with the lines
        below; 1 where it is not; 2 where it could not be decided - gh did not
        answer, printed no JSON, or the rule program could not be run - with
        the command and the message, so that the run says so, as "When a
        command does not answer" asks, and concludes nothing.
    devloop-auto-mode-question record WAY ID [DIR]   < the command
        records a refusal, from the install guard at PermissionDenied and
        from nowhere else: WAY the way of the rule the command took, ID the
        tool call's id, the command on stdin as the JSON string literal
        bin/devloop-command-reader prints on its command: line, one line.
        Exit 0 where the line stands afterwards, 2 where it could not be
        written, with the reason.
    devloop-auto-mode-question --self-test

DIR is the project directory; without it CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR, and without that
the current directory, as bin/devloop-install-record takes it.

Where the record stands. One file, devloop-auto-mode-denials, in the git
directory the project's worktrees share - git rev-parse --git-common-dir,
.git of the project in the ordinary case - so that it stands inside the
project, never in its tree and never in a commit, and nobody has to know of
it or delete it: a line that no open issue matches does nothing, and the
question ends with the issue. One line per refusal: the time in UTC, the
way, the call's id and the command as the JSON carried it, separated by
tabs. The path and the form of the line live here, and they have one other
reader: the job hooks on every system in .github/workflows/stock-take.yml
reads the record of its throwaway project and holds the line to its form,
since 22 October 2026; until 23 October 2026 this head said they lived here
and nowhere else.

What decides the question. First the rule, bin/devloop-auto-mode-rule read
beside this program, run with the interpreter running this one: exit 0, the
rule stands, ends it with 1, and gh is not called. Then the record: no git
directory, no file or no line ends it with 1, and gh is not called. Then the
open issues of the project labelled raised-here and needs-human, read with
gh issue list, and each recorded command held against the title and the body
of each issue: an issue that carries the command as the tool ran it,
verbatim, is one the question names - the skills have every such issue carry
the command as it ran, so the match is mechanical and the run's judgement
decides nothing here; an issue that carries it otherwise is not found, and
the question is not put, which is the side "In doubt, a run with nobody
there is not blocked" in docs/skill-conventions.md reads. For each issue
found, its kind: it holds a task where an open issue is blocked by it, read
with gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/N/dependencies/blocking, the
relation the readiness query of build-work reads from the other side, and
it concerns a check class otherwise, since such an issue is recorded as a
blocker of no task. gh is called in DIR, as the skills call it, each call
with 60 seconds, or with what is left of the ninety-five seconds of the
whole call where that is less, below.

The limit over the whole call, since 15 November 2026, the roadmap entry of
that date on a limit over the whole call. The program holds ninety-five
seconds over its call, counted from its start, as bin/devloop-arm and
bin/devloop-start-conditions hold theirs: a call of gh has its sixty
seconds, or what is left of the ninety-five where that is less, and none
starts once fewer than ten of them are left. So the program ends inside the
120 seconds of a Bash call, the limit of the call the texts start it in,
however many issues carry a recorded command and however slowly gh answers
for each: the last call is back at most two seconds after the ninety-five,
the two its bound may take. A call that is not started answers as one that
did not answer: not decided, with the command and the message "no time left
within the 95 seconds of this call", the words of bin/devloop-arm; one that
had less than sixty seconds and did not answer within them says "no answer
within N seconds" with the seconds it had. What the rule program and git
take, both asked before gh and neither over a network, counts on the same
clock and stands under no bound. Until that day every call had sixty
seconds and the whole call no limit, the list and one call for every issue
found, a hundred and one at most: measured on 15 November 2026 at 6f92a5d
on macOS and in alpine:3.22 with a stand-in for gh whose list answers after
50 seconds, whose first blocking relation after 55 and whose second not at
all, the program was back after 166 and 165 seconds.

How a gh that does not answer is ended, since 14 November 2026, the roadmap
entry of that date on gh under the bound. Each call of gh runs under
bin/devloop-bounded, the program beside this one, as bin/devloop-arm and
bin/devloop-start-conditions run it, with its sixty seconds, or with what is
left of the ninety-five seconds of the whole call where that is less, the
paragraph above: a gh that has not ended by then is ended with everything it
started, its process group sent SIGTERM and, where something of it stands a
second later, SIGKILL, and the bound is back at most two seconds after those
seconds with exit 124 and its line, which this program reads as no answer
within the seconds the call had. Until 16 November 2026 this paragraph gave
every call the 60 seconds and the message "no answer within 60 seconds", as
it stood since 14 November 2026, where a call has what is left of the
ninety-five since 15 November 2026.
gh's output goes to two files and through no pipe, and its input
is empty, so that a process the bound does not reach holds nothing this
program waits for: the call is back with the bound on every system. What
the bound does not reach its head says: a process that left gh's process
group on purpose, and under Windows with Git Bash a process that a process
of Git Bash under gh started and that the runtime's walk does not look for,
the block of milestone 12 on that limit in docs/defects.md. On macOS and
Linux the bound is run by its first line; a process started from Python on
Windows runs no script so, and bash, found on PATH, the one of Git Bash
where this program runs under it, runs the bound there, the call one
command line with every word in double quotes: the runtime of Git Bash
takes a command line that comes from a Windows program apart itself and
expands a pattern in a word that stands without quotes, braces among them,
and gh api is handed {owner} and {repo}; not measured here, the job hooks
on every system measures it with the self-test. Until that day each call
ran through subprocess.run with a timeout of sixty seconds, which ends the
one process it started, by SIGKILL on macOS and Linux and by
TerminateProcess under Windows, Lib/subprocess.py lines 555 to 571 of
CPython 3.14.7 and 548 to 564 of 3.12.16: what gh had started ran on,
measured on 11 and 14 November 2026 on macOS and in alpine:3.22 with a
stand-in for gh that starts a sleep and waits in a second one, both found
after the call had returned; and under Windows the output was then read to
its end without a limit, for as long as a process gh had started held it.

On exit 0, one line per key, in this order:
    question: yes
    read: 1|2             the exit of bin/devloop-auto-mode-rule read
    rule: <its line>
    issue: <number>
    kind: task|class
    title: <title>
    way: <the way of the rule the command took>
    command: <the command as it ran, a line break printed as one space>
the five lines of an issue repeated per issue, in the order of their numbers,
each issue once. On exit 1 the first line reads question: no, the second
read: 0|1|2, the third rule: <the line of read>, and a fourth says why where
the rule did not end it: denials: none recorded, or issues: none ... carries
a recorded command. On exit 2 the first line reads question: not decided,
then command: <the command that did not answer> and message: <what came
back>. record prints one line, recorded in <path>: <way>, or not recorded:
<reason>.

It needs python3, bash, git and gh, and nothing else; bash since 14 November
2026, for the bound. gh is looked up on PATH as
a shell looks a command up, through shutil.which: on Windows with the
extensions of PATHEXT, so that gh.exe and a gh.cmd beside it are found
alike, where a bare name handed to subprocess alone is found there as
gh.exe and as nothing else; where PATH holds no gh, nothing is started and
the message is the system's for a file that is not there, naming gh, since
14 November 2026; until then the name went through as it was, and the error
of the start named it on macOS and Linux and named nothing under Windows:
there the message read [WinError 2] with the text of Windows for a file
that is not found, _winapi.CreateProcess raising the error with no file
name, Modules/_winapi.c lines 1418 to 1421 of CPython 3.14.7, where on
macOS and Linux the start adds the name, Lib/subprocess.py lines 1981 to
1990; until 15 November 2026 this head said of every system that the error
of the start named it. bash is looked up on PATH under Windows alone, where
it runs the bound, above; on macOS and Linux the bound runs by its first
line, /bin/bash, whatever PATH holds. Where PATH holds no bash under
Windows, no call of gh is started and the message is the one for gh, naming
bash, since 15 November 2026; from 14 November 2026 until then the bare
name bash went into the command line there, the call was started all the
same, and the message was that [WinError 2], naming nothing. The self-test
runs every outcome against a
stand-in for gh that answers from scripted files, as the self-test of
bin/devloop-arm does: the stand-in comes first on the path, the
configuration of gh points at an empty directory, no token stands in the
environment, and nothing of it reaches GitHub; the settings of this machine
are not touched, CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR pointing under the test's own directory.
The stand-in is a Python program, run by the interpreter running the
self-test, behind the launcher the system runs off PATH - gh, a line of sh,
on macOS and Linux, and gh.cmd, a line of cmd, on Windows - so that the
self-test runs on every system, and the program finds the stand-in there as
it finds gh in operation, through the one lookup; the addendum of 22 October
2026 to the roadmap entry of that day on the question after a refusal. Until
that addendum the stand-in was a shell script, which a process started from
Python does not find on Windows, and the self-test refused there with exit 2.
The case of a gh that does not answer, since 14 November 2026: the
stand-in starts a second process and stands with it, each writing a mark
into a file of its own twenty times a second, for thirty seconds at most;
the self-test loads this program in an interpreter of its own, sets the
limit to three seconds there, five on Windows, and calls it, so that no
setting of the environment moves the limit in operation, and holds the
answer to the message with those seconds, to both files standing still
once the program has returned, and to the call being back no later than
those seconds and two after a call the stand-in answers at once.
The cases on the limit over the whole call, since 15 November 2026: where
DEVLOOP_QUESTION_CLOCK names a file, the self-test's loader puts that file
in the place of the program's clock, the seconds since its start, and the
stand-in adds to it the seconds a case gives an answer, as the stand-in of
bin/devloop-arm does, so that the cases wait for nothing; the loader alone
reads that variable, and no setting of the environment moves the clock in
operation. A call is not started with nine of the ninety-five seconds left
and is started with ten; and a call that starts with a few seconds left,
the fewest a call starts with set to those few by the loader, does not
answer and is ended after them and not after its sixty. And no bash on
PATH: under Windows no call started and the message naming bash; on macOS
and Linux, where bash is not looked up, the question decided as with one.
"""

import ast
import datetime
import errno
import json
import os
import shutil
import stat
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import time

HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))
RULE = os.path.join(HERE, "devloop-auto-mode-rule")
BOUNDED = os.path.join(HERE, "devloop-bounded")
FILE = "devloop-auto-mode-denials"
LABELS = ["raised-here", "needs-human"]
SECONDS = 60
# the limit over the whole call and the fewest seconds of it a call of gh starts with, as bin/devloop-arm holds them, since 15 November 2026
LIMIT = 95
STEP = 10
BEGAN = time.monotonic()


def now():
    """The seconds since the program began; the self-test's loader puts a
    clock of its own here."""
    return time.monotonic() - BEGAN


def usage(msg):
    print("devloop-auto-mode-question: %s" % msg, file=sys.stderr)
    return 2


def project_dir(given):
    return given or os.environ.get("CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR") or os.getcwd()


def common_dir(d):
    """The git directory the project's worktrees share, absolute, or None
    with the reason."""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", d, "rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute",
                            "--git-common-dir"], capture_output=True, text=True)
    except OSError as e:
        return None, "git could not be run: %s" % e
    if r.returncode != 0:
        return None, (r.stderr.strip() or r.stdout.strip() or
                      "git rev-parse ended with %d" % r.returncode)
    return r.stdout.strip(), None


def one_line(s):
    return " ".join(s.replace("\r\n", "\n").replace("\r", "\n").split("\n"))


def flat(s):
    """A field of the record on one line: every tab and line break a space."""
    return " ".join(s.split())


def record(way, call_id, d):
    literal = sys.stdin.read()
    literal = literal.split("\n", 1)[0].rstrip("\r")
    try:
        json.loads('"' + literal + '"')
    except ValueError:
        print("not recorded: the command on stdin is no JSON string literal")
        return 2
    cd, err = common_dir(d)
    if cd is None:
        print("not recorded: %s is no git repository: %s" % (d, err))
        return 2
    path = os.path.join(cd, FILE)
    now = datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc).strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")
    line = "%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\n" % (now, flat(way), flat(call_id), literal)
    try:
        with open(path, "a", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            f.write(line)
    except OSError as e:
        print("not recorded: %s" % e)
        return 2
    print("recorded in %s: %s" % (path, flat(way)))
    return 0


def denials(path):
    """The recorded refusals: (time, way, id, command), in order; a line not
    in the form is read over."""
    out = []
    if not os.path.exists(path):
        return out
    with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as f:
        for line in f:
            parts = line.rstrip("\n").split("\t", 3)
            if len(parts) != 4:
                continue
            try:
                cmd = json.loads('"' + parts[3] + '"')
            except ValueError:
                continue
            if cmd:
                out.append((parts[0], parts[1], parts[2], cmd))
    return out


def quoted(word):
    """A word of a Windows command line in double quotes, as
    subprocess.list2cmdline writes a word that holds a blank: a quote behind
    a backslash, and the backslashes before a quote or before the end of the
    word doubled."""
    out = []
    slashes = 0
    for c in word:
        if c == "\\":
            slashes += 1
            continue
        out.append("\\" * (slashes * 2 + 1) + c if c == '"' else "\\" * slashes + c)
        slashes = 0
    return '"' + "".join(out) + "\\" * (slashes * 2) + '"'


def under_bound(seconds, words):
    """The call that runs the words of a command under bin/devloop-bounded
    with the seconds: on macOS and Linux the bound by its first line, a
    list; on Windows one command line, bash found on PATH running the bound,
    every word in double quotes and the command's own path with forward
    slashes, a word bash reads as a path; the head says why. None under
    Windows where PATH holds no bash: the caller then starts nothing."""
    if os.name != "nt":
        return [BOUNDED, str(seconds)] + words
    bash = shutil.which("bash")
    if bash is None:  # under Windows no bash on PATH to run the bound
        return None
    return " ".join(quoted(w) for w in [bash, BOUNDED, str(seconds), words[0].replace("\\", "/")] + words[1:])


def gh(args, d):
    """The output of gh, or None with the command and the message. gh is
    found on PATH through shutil.which, as a shell finds it - on Windows with
    the extensions of PATHEXT, so that gh.exe and a gh.cmd are found alike,
    where subprocess alone would find gh.exe and nothing else - and where
    PATH holds none, nothing is started and the message names gh. It runs
    under bin/devloop-bounded with SECONDS, or with what is left of the
    LIMIT of the whole call where that is less, its output into two files
    and its input empty: a gh that does not answer is ended by the bound
    with everything it started, exit 124 and the bound's line on stderr,
    read here as no answer within the seconds it had. Where fewer than STEP
    seconds of LIMIT are left it is not started and answers as a call that
    did not answer, in the words of bin/devloop-arm; and under Windows,
    where PATH holds no bash to run the bound, it is not started and the
    message names bash."""
    shown = "gh " + " ".join(args)
    exe = shutil.which("gh")
    if exe is None:  # no gh on PATH: nothing started, the system's message for a file that is not there
        return None, shown, str(FileNotFoundError(errno.ENOENT, os.strerror(errno.ENOENT), "gh"))
    left = int(LIMIT - now())
    if left < STEP:  # fewer than STEP seconds of the whole call left: not started, answered as a call that did not answer
        return None, shown, "no time left within the %d seconds of this call" % LIMIT
    seconds = min(SECONDS, left)  # a call's own limit, or what is left of the whole call's where that is less
    call = under_bound(seconds, [exe] + args)
    if call is None:  # under Windows no bash on PATH: nothing started, the message as for gh, naming bash
        return None, shown, str(FileNotFoundError(errno.ENOENT, os.strerror(errno.ENOENT), "bash"))
    try:
        with tempfile.TemporaryFile(mode="w+") as out, tempfile.TemporaryFile(mode="w+") as err:
            rc = subprocess.run(call, cwd=d, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                                stdout=out, stderr=err).returncode
            out.seek(0)
            err.seek(0)
            r = subprocess.CompletedProcess(args, rc, out.read(), err.read())
    except OSError as e:
        return None, shown, str(e)
    if r.returncode == 124 and any(line.startswith("no answer within %d seconds: " % seconds) for line in r.stderr.split("\n")):
        return None, shown, "no answer within %d seconds" % seconds
    if r.returncode != 0:
        return None, shown, (r.stderr.strip() or r.stdout.strip() or
                             "exit %d with nothing printed" % r.returncode)
    return r.stdout, shown, None


def not_decided(command, message):
    print("question: not decided")
    print("command: %s" % one_line(command))
    print("message: %s" % one_line(message))
    return 2


def question(d):
    try:
        r = subprocess.run([sys.executable, RULE, "read"], capture_output=True,
                           text=True)
    except OSError as e:
        return not_decided("%s read" % RULE, str(e))
    rule_line = one_line(r.stdout.strip() or r.stderr.strip())
    if r.returncode == 0:
        print("question: no")
        print("read: 0")
        print("rule: %s" % rule_line)
        return 1
    if r.returncode not in (1, 2):
        return not_decided("%s read" % RULE,
                           "ended with %d: %s" % (r.returncode, rule_line))
    head = ["read: %d" % r.returncode, "rule: %s" % rule_line]

    def no(why):
        print("question: no")
        for h in head:
            print(h)
        print(why)
        return 1

    cd, err = common_dir(d)
    if cd is None:
        return no("denials: none can stand, %s is no git repository: %s"
                  % (d, one_line(err)))
    recorded = denials(os.path.join(cd, FILE))
    if not recorded:
        return no("denials: none recorded")
    out, shown, err = gh(["issue", "list", "--state", "open",
                          "--label", LABELS[0], "--label", LABELS[1],
                          "--limit", "100", "--json", "number,title,body"], d)
    if out is None:
        return not_decided(shown, err)
    try:
        issues = json.loads(out)
        if not isinstance(issues, list):
            raise ValueError("not a list")
    except ValueError as e:
        return not_decided(shown, "printed no JSON list of issues: %s" % e)
    found = []
    for issue in sorted((i for i in issues if isinstance(i, dict)),
                        key=lambda i: i.get("number") or 0):
        number = issue.get("number")
        if not isinstance(number, int):
            continue
        text = (issue.get("title") or "") + "\n" + (issue.get("body") or "")
        for _, way, _, cmd in recorded:
            if cmd in text:
                found.append((number, issue.get("title") or "", way, cmd))
                break
    if not found:
        return no("issues: none of the %d open issues labelled %s and %s "
                  "carries a recorded command" % (len(issues), LABELS[0], LABELS[1]))
    lines = []
    for number, title, way, cmd in found:
        out, shown, err = gh(["api", "repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/%d/dependencies/blocking"
                              % number], d)
        if out is None:
            return not_decided(shown, err)
        try:
            blocking = json.loads(out)
            if not isinstance(blocking, list):
                raise ValueError("not a list")
        except ValueError as e:
            return not_decided(shown, "printed no JSON list of issues: %s" % e)
        kind = "task" if any(isinstance(b, dict) and b.get("state") == "open"
                             for b in blocking) else "class"
        lines += ["issue: %d" % number, "kind: %s" % kind,
                  "title: %s" % one_line(title), "way: %s" % way,
                  "command: %s" % one_line(cmd)]
    print("question: yes")
    for h in head:
        print(h)
    for line in lines:
        print(line)
    return 0


# ------------------------------------------------------------- the self-test

# The stand-in for gh of the self-test: a Python program, which the launcher
# the system runs off PATH hands every argument - gh, a line of sh, on macOS
# and Linux; gh.cmd, a line of cmd, on Windows, where the lookup of the
# program finds it through PATHEXT as it finds gh.exe. It answers the issue
# list and the blocking relation of one issue from scripted files under
# $DEVLOOP_QUESTION_SCRIPT, logs every call, and marks a call no answer was
# scripted for. Where a file .hang stands beside the scripted answer it does
# not answer: it starts a second process and stands with it, each writing a
# mark into a file of its own twenty times a second, thirty seconds at most,
# so that the self-test reads off the two files whether either still stands.
# Where a file .t stands beside the scripted answer it adds the seconds in it
# to the self-test's clock, the file $DEVLOOP_QUESTION_CLOCK names, before it
# answers, as the stand-in for gh of bin/devloop-arm does.
STAND_IN = r'''import os
import re
import sys
S = os.environ["DEVLOOP_QUESTION_SCRIPT"]
args = " ".join(sys.argv[1:])
with open(os.path.join(S, "log"), "a", encoding="utf-8") as f:
    f.write(args + "\n")
m = re.match(r"api repos/\{owner\}/\{repo\}/issues/([0-9]+)/dependencies/blocking$", args)
if args.startswith("issue list "):
    a = os.path.join(S, "issues")
elif m:
    a = os.path.join(S, "blocking." + m.group(1))
else:
    a = os.path.join(S, "other")
if os.path.exists(a + ".hang"):
    import subprocess
    import time
    marks = "import sys, time\nfor i in range(600):\n    open(sys.argv[1], 'a').write('.')\n    time.sleep(0.05)\n"
    subprocess.Popen([sys.executable, "-c", marks, os.path.join(S, "marks.started")])
    for i in range(600):
        open(os.path.join(S, "marks.gh"), "a").write(".")
        time.sleep(0.05)
    sys.exit(3)
if not os.path.exists(a + ".rc"):
    sys.stderr.write("stand-in for gh: no answer scripted for: gh %s\n" % args)
    open(os.path.join(S, "unscripted"), "w").close()
    sys.exit(1)
if os.path.exists(a + ".t"):
    clock = os.environ["DEVLOOP_QUESTION_CLOCK"]
    told = float(open(clock, encoding="utf-8").read()) + float(open(a + ".t", encoding="utf-8").read())
    open(clock, "w", encoding="utf-8").write("%s\n" % told)
sys.stdout.write(open(a + ".out", encoding="utf-8").read())
sys.stderr.write(open(a + ".err", encoding="utf-8").read())
sys.exit(int(open(a + ".rc", encoding="utf-8").read().strip()))
'''
LAUNCHER_SH = '#!/bin/sh\nexec "%s" "%s" "$@"\n'
LAUNCHER_CMD = '@"%s" "%%~dp0gh-stand-in.py" %%*\r\n'
# The program loaded in an interpreter of its own, for the cases of the
# self-test that need what no call from outside sets: the limits named in
# the second argument, SECONDS=3 or STEP=3, several joined by a comma, a
# command taken off the lookup where the third argument names it, gh or
# bash, and, where DEVLOOP_QUESTION_CLOCK names a file, the seconds in that
# file in the place of the program's clock. No setting of the environment
# moves a limit or the clock in operation: the loader alone reads that
# variable.
LOADER = """import os
import shutil
import sys
p, limits, gone = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2], sys.argv[3]
g = {"__name__": "devloop_auto_mode_question", "__file__": p}
exec(compile(open(p, encoding="utf-8").read(), p, "exec"), g)
for limit in limits.split(","):
    g[limit.split("=")[0]] = int(limit.split("=")[1])
clock = os.environ.get("DEVLOOP_QUESTION_CLOCK")
if clock:  # the self-test's clock in the place of the program's
    g["now"] = lambda: float(open(clock, encoding="utf-8").read())
found = shutil.which
shutil.which = lambda name, *a, **k: None if name == gone else found(name, *a, **k)
sys.exit(g["main"]([p] + sys.argv[4:]))
"""


def same(a, b):
    """Whether two paths name one place, as the system reads them."""
    return os.path.normcase(os.path.realpath(a)) == os.path.normcase(os.path.realpath(b))


def selftest():
    me = os.path.realpath(__file__)
    # the real path, since /var is a link to /private/var on macOS
    T = os.path.realpath(tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="devloop-auto-mode-question."))
    n = [0]
    try:
        os.makedirs(os.path.join(T, "bin"))
        os.makedirs(os.path.join(T, "gh-config"))
        os.makedirs(os.path.join(T, "config"))
        os.makedirs(os.path.join(T, "s"))
        os.makedirs(os.path.join(T, "p"))
        os.makedirs(os.path.join(T, "nogit"))
        stand_in = os.path.join(T, "bin", "gh-stand-in.py")
        with open(stand_in, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            f.write(STAND_IN)
        if os.name == "nt":
            gh_path = os.path.join(T, "bin", "gh.cmd")
            with open(gh_path, "w", encoding="utf-8", newline="") as f:
                f.write(LAUNCHER_CMD % sys.executable)
        else:
            gh_path = os.path.join(T, "bin", "gh")
            with open(gh_path, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
                f.write(LAUNCHER_SH % (sys.executable, stand_in))
            os.chmod(gh_path, os.stat(gh_path).st_mode | stat.S_IXUSR | stat.S_IXGRP | stat.S_IXOTH)
        subprocess.run(["git", "init", "-q", os.path.join(T, "p")], check=True)
        env = dict(os.environ)
        for k in ("GH_TOKEN", "GITHUB_TOKEN", "GH_ENTERPRISE_TOKEN",
                  "GITHUB_ENTERPRISE_TOKEN", "GH_HOST", "CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR"):
            env.pop(k, None)
        env["PATH"] = os.path.join(T, "bin") + os.pathsep + env.get("PATH", "")
        env["GH_CONFIG_DIR"] = os.path.join(T, "gh-config")
        env["CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR"] = os.path.join(T, "config")
        env["DEVLOOP_QUESTION_SCRIPT"] = os.path.join(T, "s")
        found = shutil.which("gh", path=env["PATH"])
        if found is None or not same(found, gh_path):
            print("FAIL the stand-in for gh is not the gh the path finds: %s" % found)
            return 1
        S = os.path.join(T, "s")
        settings = os.path.join(T, "config", "settings.json")
        denials_file = os.path.join(T, "p", ".git", FILE)

        def fresh():
            for name in os.listdir(S):
                os.unlink(os.path.join(S, name))
            open(os.path.join(S, "log"), "w").close()
            if os.path.exists(settings):
                os.unlink(settings)
            if os.path.exists(denials_file):
                os.unlink(denials_file)

        def ans(kind, rc, out, err=""):
            for suffix, content in ((".rc", "%d\n" % rc), (".out", out), (".err", err)):
                with open(os.path.join(S, kind + suffix), "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
                    f.write(content)

        def issues(*items):
            ans("issues", 0, json.dumps([{"number": num, "title": title, "body": body}
                                         for num, title, body in items]))

        def blocking(num, *states):
            ans("blocking.%d" % num, 0, json.dumps(
                [{"number": 900 + i, "state": s} for i, s in enumerate(states)]))

        def run(*args, stdin=""):
            r = subprocess.run([sys.executable, me] + list(args), cwd=os.path.join(T, "p"),
                               input=stdin, capture_output=True, text=True, env=env)
            return r.returncode, r.stdout + r.stderr

        def loaded(limits, gone, *args, clock=None):
            """the program through LOADER: (exit code, output) and the
            seconds the call took; with clock, the program on the
            self-test's clock, a file that starts at those seconds"""
            e = dict(env)
            if clock is not None:  # the self-test's clock, which the stand-in adds to
                e["DEVLOOP_QUESTION_CLOCK"] = os.path.join(S, "clock")
                with open(e["DEVLOOP_QUESTION_CLOCK"], "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
                    f.write("%d\n" % clock)
            began = time.monotonic()
            r = subprocess.run([sys.executable, "-c", LOADER, me, limits, gone] + list(args),
                               cwd=os.path.join(T, "p"), capture_output=True, text=True, env=e)
            return (r.returncode, r.stdout + r.stderr), time.monotonic() - began

        def takes(kind, seconds):
            """the scripted answer of that kind takes the seconds on the self-test's clock"""
            with open(os.path.join(S, kind + ".t"), "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
                f.write("%d\n" % seconds)

        def calls():
            with open(os.path.join(S, "log"), encoding="utf-8") as f:
                return [l.strip() for l in f if l.strip()]

        def expect(name, got, want_rc, *want_lines):
            rc, out = got
            n[0] += 1
            lines = out.split("\n")
            if rc != want_rc:
                print("FAIL %s: exit %d, expected %d" % (name, rc, want_rc))
                print("    " + "\n    ".join(lines))
                return False
            for w in want_lines:
                if w not in lines:
                    print("FAIL %s: no line %r" % (name, w))
                    print("    " + "\n    ".join(lines))
                    return False
            if os.path.exists(os.path.join(S, "unscripted")):
                print("FAIL %s: the stand-in was called for something no case scripted" % name)
                print("    " + "\n    ".join(lines))
                return False
            print("ok   %s" % name)
            return True

        def absent(name, got, s):
            if s in got[1]:
                print("FAIL %s: %r stands there and should not" % (name, s))
                return False
            return True

        def standing_still(name, *files):
            """every file of marks there, so that its writer had started, and
            none longer half a second later, so that no writer stands"""
            n[0] += 1
            paths = [os.path.join(S, f) for f in files]
            before = [os.path.getsize(q) if os.path.exists(q) else -1 for q in paths]
            time.sleep(0.5)
            after = [os.path.getsize(q) if os.path.exists(q) else -1 for q in paths]
            for f, b, a in zip(files, before, after):
                if b < 1:
                    print("FAIL %s: %s was never written, its writer had not started" % (name, f))
                    return False
                if a != b:
                    print("FAIL %s: %s grew from %d to %d marks in half a second, its writer still stands" % (name, f, b, a))
                    return False
            print("ok   %s" % name)
            return True

        def recorded(name, got, way):
            """exit 0 and the line recorded in <the record>: <way>, the path
            read as the system reads it, since git prints it with the
            slashes of its own on Windows"""
            rc, out = got
            n[0] += 1
            for line in out.split("\n"):
                if rc == 0 and line.startswith("recorded in ") and line.endswith(": " + way):
                    if same(line[len("recorded in "):-len(": " + way)], denials_file):
                        print("ok   %s" % name)
                        return True
            print("FAIL %s: exit %d, no line recorded in %s: %s" % (name, rc, denials_file, way))
            print("    " + "\n    ".join(out.split("\n")))
            return False

        def rule_stands():
            r = subprocess.run([sys.executable, RULE, "write"], capture_output=True,
                               text=True, env=env)
            assert r.returncode == 0, r.stdout + r.stderr

        def rule_unreadable():
            with open(settings, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
                f.write("{ not json\n")

        ok = True
        lit = "brew install gitleaks"
        lit2 = "npx playwright install chromium"
        # the arguments
        ok &= expect("an argument it does not know: exit 2", run("--nope"), 2,
                     "devloop-auto-mode-question: expected a project directory, "
                     "record WAY ID [DIR] with the command on stdin, or --self-test, got --nope")
        ok &= expect("two directories: exit 2", run("a", "b"), 2,
                     "devloop-auto-mode-question: expected at most one directory, got 2")
        ok &= expect("record with one argument: exit 2", run("record", "brew install"), 2,
                     "devloop-auto-mode-question: record expects WAY and ID, then at most one directory")
        # record
        fresh()
        ok &= recorded("record: the line written under .git, exit 0",
                       run("record", "brew install", "toolu_1", stdin=lit + "\n"), "brew install")
        with open(denials_file, encoding="utf-8") as f:
            text = f.read()
        parts = text.rstrip("\n").split("\t")
        n[0] += 1
        if len(parts) == 4 and parts[1] == "brew install" and parts[2] == "toolu_1" and parts[3] == lit and parts[0].endswith("Z"):
            print("ok   record: four fields, the time in UTC, the way, the id, the literal")
        else:
            ok = False
            print("FAIL record: the line reads %r" % text)
        ok &= recorded("record of a second refusal appends a line",
                       run("record", "playwright install", "toolu_2", stdin=lit2 + "\n"), "playwright install")
        with open(denials_file, encoding="utf-8") as f:
            n[0] += 1
            if len(f.read().rstrip("\n").split("\n")) == 2:
                print("ok   record: two lines stand")
            else:
                ok = False
                print("FAIL record: not two lines")
        ok &= recorded("record with a way carrying a tab and a line break: flattened to spaces",
                       run("record", "brew\tinstall\nx", "tool u", stdin=lit + "\n"), "brew install x")
        ok &= expect("record of a literal that is no JSON string: not recorded, exit 2",
                     run("record", "brew install", "toolu_3", stdin='a "quote\n'), 2,
                     "not recorded: the command on stdin is no JSON string literal")
        ok &= expect("record outside a git repository: not recorded, exit 2",
                     run("record", "brew install", "toolu_3", os.path.join(T, "nogit"), stdin=lit + "\n"), 2)
        n[0] += 1
        if "not recorded: %s is no git repository" % os.path.join(T, "nogit") in run("record", "brew install", "t", os.path.join(T, "nogit"), stdin=lit + "\n")[1]:
            print("ok   record outside a git repository names the directory")
        else:
            ok = False
            print("FAIL record outside a git repository does not name the directory")
        # the question: the rule stands
        fresh(); rule_stands()
        ok &= expect("the rule stands: no question, gh not called, exit 1", run(), 1,
                     "question: no", "read: 0")
        n[0] += 1
        if calls() == []:
            print("ok   the rule stands: gh not called")
        else:
            ok = False
            print("FAIL the rule stands: gh called: %r" % calls())
        # the rule missing, nothing recorded
        fresh()
        ok &= expect("the rule missing and no refusal recorded: no question, exit 1", run(), 1,
                     "question: no", "read: 1", "denials: none recorded")
        n[0] += 1
        if calls() == []:
            print("ok   nothing recorded: gh not called")
        else:
            ok = False
            print("FAIL nothing recorded: gh called: %r" % calls())
        # recorded, no open issue carrying it
        fresh(); run("record", "brew install", "toolu_1", stdin=lit + "\n")
        issues((5, "Something else", "no install here"))
        ok &= expect("a refusal recorded and no open issue carrying the command: no question, exit 1", run(), 1,
                     "question: no", "read: 1",
                     "issues: none of the 1 open issues labelled raised-here and needs-human carries a recorded command")
        # recorded, an issue holding a task
        issues((12, "Install gitleaks for task #7", "Auto mode refused:\n\n    brew install gitleaks\n\nat ~/bin nothing stands."))
        blocking(12, "open")
        open(os.path.join(S, "log"), "w").close()
        ok &= expect("the rule missing and an open issue holding a task: question, kind task, exit 0", run(), 0,
                     "question: yes", "read: 1", "issue: 12", "kind: task",
                     "title: Install gitleaks for task #7", "way: brew install", "command: brew install gitleaks")
        n[0] += 1
        if calls() == ["issue list --state open --label raised-here --label needs-human --limit 100 --json number,title,body",
                       "api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/12/dependencies/blocking"]:
            print("ok   the issue list read once and the blocking relation of the one issue")
        else:
            ok = False
            print("FAIL the calls were %r" % calls())
        # an issue concerning a check class
        fresh(); run("record", "brew install", "toolu_1", stdin=lit + "\n")
        issues((15, "The class secrets: auto mode refused the install", "`brew install gitleaks`"))
        blocking(15)
        ok &= expect("the rule missing and an open issue concerning a check class: question, kind class", run(), 0,
                     "question: yes", "issue: 15", "kind: class", "way: brew install")
        # the blocked task closed: no open issue is blocked, kind class
        blocking(15, "closed")
        ok &= expect("an issue whose blocked task is closed: kind class", run(), 0, "issue: 15", "kind: class")
        # both kinds open
        fresh(); run("record", "brew install", "toolu_1", stdin=lit + "\n"); run("record", "playwright install", "toolu_2", stdin=lit2 + "\n")
        issues((15, "The class", "`brew install gitleaks`"), (12, "The task", "npx playwright install chromium\n"), (3, "Other", "nothing"))
        blocking(12, "closed", "open"); blocking(15)
        got = run()
        ok &= expect("both kinds open: question naming both, in the order of their numbers", got, 0,
                     "question: yes", "issue: 12", "kind: task", "way: playwright install",
                     "command: npx playwright install chromium", "issue: 15", "kind: class")
        n[0] += 1
        if got[1].index("issue: 12") < got[1].index("issue: 15"):
            print("ok   both kinds open: the lower number first")
        else:
            ok = False
            print("FAIL both kinds open: the order is wrong")
        # the same command in two issues: both named, each once
        issues((15, "The class", "`brew install gitleaks`"), (16, "Again", "brew install gitleaks"))
        blocking(15); blocking(16)
        got = run()
        ok &= expect("one command carried by two open issues: both named", got, 0, "issue: 15", "issue: 16")
        n[0] += 1
        if got[1].count("issue: 15") == 1:
            print("ok   one command carried by two open issues: each once")
        else:
            ok = False
            print("FAIL an issue named twice")
        # the earlier wording of the rule standing, since 23 October 2026: read
        # ends with 1, so that the question comes again, here after a refusal
        # by a way the earlier wording did not name; the wording read off the
        # rule program's source, which nothing here imports
        with open(RULE, encoding="utf-8") as f:
            earlier = [ast.literal_eval(node.value) for node in ast.parse(f.read()).body
                       if isinstance(node, ast.Assign) and any(getattr(t, "id", "") == "EARLIER" for t in node.targets)][0]
        fresh()
        with open(settings, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            json.dump({"autoMode": {"allow": ["$defaults", earlier[0]]}}, f)
        run("record", "npm install -g", "toolu_5", stdin="npm install -g gitleaks\n")
        issues((17, "The class", "npm install -g gitleaks")); blocking(17)
        ok &= expect("the earlier wording of the rule standing: read 1, the question, the way npm install -g", run(), 0,
                     "question: yes", "read: 1",
                     "rule: the rule does not stand in %s: the list autoMode.allow carries an earlier wording of it, which allows less" % settings,
                     "issue: 17", "way: npm install -g", "command: npm install -g gitleaks")
        # read ends with 2
        fresh(); rule_unreadable(); run("record", "brew install", "toolu_1", stdin=lit + "\n")
        issues((15, "The class", "brew install gitleaks")); blocking(15)
        ok &= expect("the settings unreadable, read 2, and an open issue: question, read: 2", run(), 0,
                     "question: yes", "read: 2", "issue: 15")
        # the issue closed
        fresh(); run("record", "brew install", "toolu_1", stdin=lit + "\n")
        issues()
        ok &= expect("the issue closed, the list empty: no question, exit 1", run(), 1,
                     "question: no", "issues: none of the 0 open issues labelled raised-here and needs-human carries a recorded command")
        # after a no, the issue still open: the question again, nothing written
        issues((15, "The class", "brew install gitleaks")); blocking(15)
        before = open(denials_file, encoding="utf-8").read()
        ok &= expect("after a no the issue still open: the question again", run(), 0, "question: yes", "issue: 15")
        ok &= expect("and again", run(), 0, "question: yes", "issue: 15")
        n[0] += 1
        if open(denials_file, encoding="utf-8").read() == before and not os.path.exists(settings):
            print("ok   the question writes nothing: the record and the settings as before")
        else:
            ok = False
            print("FAIL the question wrote something")
        # a command carried in the title alone
        issues((15, "brew install gitleaks refused", "")); blocking(15)
        ok &= expect("the command in the title alone: found", run(), 0, "issue: 15")
        # a command carried otherwise: not found
        issues((15, "The class", "brew  install gitleaks"))
        ok &= expect("the command carried otherwise, two blanks: not found, no question", run(), 1, "question: no")
        # a multi-line command
        fresh(); run("record", "cargo install", "toolu_9", stdin="cargo install \\\\\\n  ripgrep\n")
        issues((20, "Task", "    cargo install \\\n  ripgrep\n")); blocking(20, "open")
        ok &= expect("a command over two lines: matched verbatim, printed on one line", run(), 0,
                     "issue: 20", "command: cargo install \\   ripgrep")
        # a line of the record not in the form
        fresh()
        with open(denials_file, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            f.write("garbage\n")
        issues((15, "The class", "brew install gitleaks"))
        ok &= expect("a record whose lines are not in the form: as none recorded", run(), 1, "denials: none recorded")
        # gh does not answer
        fresh(); run("record", "brew install", "toolu_1", stdin=lit + "\n")
        ans("issues", 1, "", "HTTP 502: Bad Gateway (https://api.github.com/graphql)")
        ok &= expect("gh issue list fails: not decided, the command and the message, exit 2", run(), 2,
                     "question: not decided",
                     "command: gh issue list --state open --label raised-here --label needs-human --limit 100 --json number,title,body",
                     "message: HTTP 502: Bad Gateway (https://api.github.com/graphql)")
        ans("issues", 0, "not json")
        # the message is what json.loads says of the stand-in's text, read off this interpreter, since its wording is the interpreter's and the case is to come out alike on every system
        try:
            json.loads("not json")
        except ValueError as e:
            no_json = str(e)
        ok &= expect("gh prints no JSON: not decided, the command and the message", run(), 2,
                     "question: not decided",
                     "command: gh issue list --state open --label raised-here --label needs-human --limit 100 --json number,title,body",
                     "message: printed no JSON list of issues: %s" % no_json)
        issues((15, "The class", "brew install gitleaks"))
        ans("blocking.15", 1, "", "HTTP 404: Not Found")
        ok &= expect("the blocking relation does not answer: not decided", run(), 2,
                     "question: not decided", "command: gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/15/dependencies/blocking",
                     "message: HTTP 404: Not Found")
        # gh does not answer within the limit, a few seconds here, more on Windows, where the processes of the stand-in take longer to start: ended by the bound with the process it started, the message with the seconds, and the call back no later than those seconds and two after a call gh answers at once
        short = 5 if os.name == "nt" else 3
        fresh(); run("record", "brew install", "toolu_1", stdin=lit + "\n")
        ans("issues", 1, "", "HTTP 502: Bad Gateway (https://api.github.com/graphql)")
        got, at_once = loaded("SECONDS=%d" % short, "")
        ok &= expect("the limit set to a few seconds and gh answering at once: as with sixty", got, 2,
                     "question: not decided",
                     "message: HTTP 502: Bad Gateway (https://api.github.com/graphql)")
        open(os.path.join(S, "issues.hang"), "w").close()
        got, hung = loaded("SECONDS=%d" % short, "")
        ok &= expect("gh does not answer within the limit: not decided, the command, no answer within the seconds, exit 2", got, 2,
                     "question: not decided",
                     "command: gh issue list --state open --label raised-here --label needs-human --limit 100 --json number,title,body",
                     "message: no answer within %d seconds" % short)
        ok &= standing_still("gh does not answer within the limit: ended with the process it started, neither standing once the program has returned",
                             "marks.gh", "marks.started")
        n[0] += 1
        if hung <= at_once + short + 2:
            print("ok   gh does not answer within the limit: back no later than the limit and two seconds after a call answered at once")
        else:
            ok = False
            print("FAIL gh does not answer within the limit: back after %.3f seconds, %.3f where gh answers at once, more than the %d seconds and 2 apart" % (hung, at_once, short))
        # no gh on PATH: nothing started, the system's message for a file that is not there
        fresh(); run("record", "brew install", "toolu_1", stdin=lit + "\n")
        got, _ = loaded("SECONDS=%d" % short, "gh")
        ok &= expect("no gh on PATH: not decided, the command and the system's message naming gh, exit 2", got, 2,
                     "question: not decided",
                     "command: gh issue list --state open --label raised-here --label needs-human --limit 100 --json number,title,body",
                     "message: %s" % FileNotFoundError(errno.ENOENT, os.strerror(errno.ENOENT), "gh"))
        n[0] += 1
        if calls() == []:
            print("ok   no gh on PATH: nothing started")
        else:
            ok = False
            print("FAIL no gh on PATH: gh called: %r" % calls())
        # the limit over the whole call, on the self-test's clock: no call of gh starts once fewer than ten of the ninety-five seconds are left, and a call that is not started answers as one that did not answer
        listing = "issue list --state open --label raised-here --label needs-human --limit 100 --json number,title,body"
        fresh(); run("record", "brew install", "toolu_1", stdin=lit + "\n")
        issues((15, "The class", "brew install gitleaks"), (16, "Again", "brew install gitleaks")); blocking(15); blocking(16)
        takes("issues", 50); takes("blocking.15", 36)
        got, _ = loaded("SECONDS=60", "", clock=0)
        ok &= expect("the list after 50 seconds and the first blocking relation after 36 more, nine of the 95 left: the second not started, not decided, the command and no time left, exit 2", got, 2,
                     "question: not decided",
                     "command: gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/16/dependencies/blocking",
                     "message: no time left within the 95 seconds of this call")
        n[0] += 1
        if calls() == [listing, "api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/15/dependencies/blocking"]:
            print("ok   nine of the 95 seconds left: the call of gh not started")
        else:
            ok = False
            print("FAIL nine of the 95 seconds left: the calls were %r" % calls())
        fresh(); run("record", "brew install", "toolu_1", stdin=lit + "\n")
        issues((15, "The class", "brew install gitleaks"), (16, "Again", "brew install gitleaks")); blocking(15); blocking(16)
        takes("issues", 50); takes("blocking.15", 35)
        got, _ = loaded("SECONDS=60", "", clock=0)
        ok &= expect("the list after 50 seconds and the first blocking relation after 35 more, ten of the 95 left: the second asked, the question decided, exit 0", got, 0,
                     "question: yes", "issue: 15", "issue: 16")
        # a call that starts with less than its sixty seconds left has what is left: the fewest seconds a call starts with set to a few, the list answering with those few of the ninety-five left, the blocking relation not answering: ended after those few and not after sixty, the message with them
        fresh(); run("record", "brew install", "toolu_1", stdin=lit + "\n")
        issues((15, "The class", "brew install gitleaks")); takes("issues", 95 - short)
        open(os.path.join(S, "blocking.15.hang"), "w").close()
        got, hung = loaded("SECONDS=60,STEP=%d" % short, "", clock=0)
        ok &= expect("a call started with a few of the 95 seconds left has those and not its sixty: not decided, the command, no answer within those seconds, exit 2", got, 2,
                     "question: not decided",
                     "command: gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/15/dependencies/blocking",
                     "message: no answer within %d seconds" % short)
        ok &= standing_still("a call started with a few of the 95 seconds left: ended with the process it started, neither standing once the program has returned",
                             "marks.gh", "marks.started")
        n[0] += 1
        if hung - at_once <= short + 2:
            print("ok   a call started with a few of the 95 seconds left: back no later than those seconds and two after a call answered at once")
        else:
            ok = False
            print("FAIL a call started with a few of the 95 seconds left: back after %.3f seconds, %.3f where gh answers at once, more than the %d seconds and 2 apart" % (hung, at_once, short))
        # no bash on PATH: under Windows, where bash runs the bound, no call of gh is started and the message names bash; on macOS and Linux bash is not looked up, the bound running by its first line, and the question is decided as with one
        fresh(); run("record", "brew install", "toolu_1", stdin=lit + "\n")
        issues((15, "The class", "brew install gitleaks")); blocking(15)
        got, _ = loaded("SECONDS=%d" % short, "bash")
        under_windows = os.name == "nt"
        if under_windows:
            ok &= expect("no bash on PATH under Windows: not decided, the command and the system's message naming bash, exit 2", got, 2,
                         "question: not decided", "command: gh " + listing,
                         "message: %s" % FileNotFoundError(errno.ENOENT, os.strerror(errno.ENOENT), "bash"))
        else:
            ok &= expect("no bash on PATH on macOS and Linux: bash not looked up, the question decided as with one, exit 0", got, 0,
                         "question: yes", "issue: 15")
        n[0] += 1
        if calls() == ([] if under_windows else [listing, "api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/15/dependencies/blocking"]):
            print("ok   no bash on PATH: nothing started under Windows, gh called as with one on macOS and Linux")
        else:
            ok = False
            print("FAIL no bash on PATH: the calls were %r" % calls())
        # a project that is no git repository
        ok &= expect("a directory that is no git repository: no question", run(os.path.join(T, "nogit")), 1,
                     "question: no")
        # CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR
        fresh(); run("record", "brew install", "toolu_1", stdin=lit + "\n")
        issues((15, "The class", "brew install gitleaks")); blocking(15)
        env2 = dict(env); env2["CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR"] = os.path.join(T, "p")
        r = subprocess.run([sys.executable, me], cwd=T, capture_output=True, text=True, env=env2)
        ok &= expect("the project from CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR where no directory is given", (r.returncode, r.stdout + r.stderr), 0, "issue: 15")
        if ok:
            print("SELF-TEST PASSED: %d cases" % n[0])
            return 0
        print("SELF-TEST FAILED")
        return 1
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(T, ignore_errors=True)


def main(argv):
    args = argv[1:]
    if args == ["--self-test"]:
        return selftest()
    if args and args[0] == "record":
        rest = args[1:]
        if len(rest) < 2 or len(rest) > 3:
            return usage("record expects WAY and ID, then at most one directory")
        return record(rest[0], rest[1], project_dir(rest[2] if len(rest) == 3 else ""))
    dirs = [a for a in args if not a.startswith("-")]
    bad = [a for a in args if a.startswith("-")]
    if bad:
        return usage("expected a project directory, record WAY ID [DIR] with the "
                     "command on stdin, or --self-test, got %s" % bad[0])
    if len(dirs) > 1:
        return usage("expected at most one directory, got %d" % len(dirs))
    return question(project_dir(dirs[0] if dirs else ""))


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv))
